WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.141
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book three.

2
00:00:05.441 --> 00:00:09.188
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.488 --> 00:00:15.901
Unit four: Cognitive Science. Today you will explain limits using constrained by / limited by.

4
00:00:16.201 --> 00:00:18.714
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.667 --> 00:00:22.795
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.650 --> 00:00:24.628
Cognition.

7
00:00:25.617 --> 00:00:29.393
Cognition means the mental processes of knowing, thinking and remembering.

8
00:00:29.983 --> 00:00:32.343
Sleep strongly affects cognition.

9
00:00:32.950 --> 00:00:35.428
Your turn. Say: Cognition.

10
00:00:38.267 --> 00:00:39.384
Cognition.

11
00:00:40.150 --> 00:00:41.708
Mental representation.

12
00:00:42.717 --> 00:00:46.585
Mental representation means an internal model of something in the world.

13
00:00:47.183 --> 00:00:50.657
A map in your head is a mental representation.

14
00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:54.402
Your turn. Say: Mental representation.

15
00:00:57.267 --> 00:00:58.918
Mental representation.

16
00:00:59.683 --> 00:01:00.789
Neural network.

17
00:01:01.783 --> 00:01:06.128
Neural network means a web of connected neurons, or a computer model of one.

18
00:01:06.717 --> 00:01:09.715
A neural network can learn to recognise faces.

19
00:01:10.317 --> 00:01:13.317
Your turn. Say: Neural network.

20
00:01:16.167 --> 00:01:17.748
Neural network.

21
00:01:18.483 --> 00:01:19.601
Perception.

22
00:01:20.617 --> 00:01:23.917
Perception means the interpretation of sensory information.

23
00:01:24.517 --> 00:01:26.888
Perception is shaped by expectation.

24
00:01:27.483 --> 00:01:29.927
Your turn. Say: Perception.

25
00:01:32.767 --> 00:01:33.733
Perception.

26
00:01:34.483 --> 00:01:35.844
Memory encoding.

27
00:01:36.850 --> 00:01:41.125
Memory encoding means the process of turning experience into a storable memory.

28
00:01:41.717 --> 00:01:45.005
Memory encoding improves when material is meaningful.

29
00:01:45.617 --> 00:01:48.652
Your turn. Say: Memory encoding.

30
00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:53.256
Memory encoding.

31
00:01:54.017 --> 00:01:55.134
Attention.

32
00:01:56.150 --> 00:01:59.543
Attention means the selective focus of mental resources.

33
00:02:00.150 --> 00:02:02.359
Divided attention reduces learning.

34
00:02:02.950 --> 00:02:05.498
Your turn. Say: Attention.

35
00:02:08.333 --> 00:02:09.033
Attention.

36
00:02:09.783 --> 00:02:11.028
Cognitive load.

37
00:02:12.017 --> 00:02:16.118
Cognitive load means the amount of mental effort used in working memory.

38
00:02:16.717 --> 00:02:19.726
Too many slides raised the audience's cognitive load.

39
00:02:20.317 --> 00:02:23.224
Your turn. Say: Cognitive load.

40
00:02:26.067 --> 00:02:27.370
Cognitive load.

41
00:02:28.117 --> 00:02:29.733
Information processing.

42
00:02:30.717 --> 00:02:34.945
Information processing means the way the mind takes in and transforms information.

43
00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:38.560
Information processing slows when we multitask.

44
00:02:39.150 --> 00:02:42.151
Your turn. Say: Information processing.

45
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:46.988
Information processing.

46
00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:51.149
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:51.983 --> 00:02:56.470
Visal, I studied for six hours but remember almost nothing. Why?

48
00:02:57.583 --> 00:03:05.565
Learning is often constrained by cognitive load. Working memory is limited by how many items it can hold at once.

49
00:03:06.650 --> 00:03:08.626
So studying longer isn't the answer?

50
00:03:09.717 --> 00:03:18.023
No. Attention is limited by fatigue, and memory encoding is constrained by how meaningful the material feels.

51
00:03:19.117 --> 00:03:20.199
What helps?

52
00:03:21.283 --> 00:03:30.112
Chunking, spacing and testing. Each reduces load, so information processing is less constrained by working memory.

53
00:03:31.267 --> 00:03:32.686
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:33.550 --> 00:03:36.618
Grammar focus: Constrained by and limited by.

55
00:03:36.918 --> 00:03:38.151
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:38.850 --> 00:03:41.709
Learning is often constrained by cognitive load.

57
00:03:42.617 --> 00:03:46.044
Working memory is limited by how many items it can hold.

58
00:03:46.950 --> 00:03:49.054
Attention is bounded by fatigue.

59
00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:58.643
Passive + by names the limit: constrained by, limited by, bounded by, restricted by. The limiting factor is the agent after 'by'.

60
00:03:59.383 --> 00:04:03.115
Be careful! Don't say: Learning constrained by load.

61
00:04:05.067 --> 00:04:07.891
Say: Learning is constrained by load.

62
00:04:08.191 --> 00:04:09.633
Passive needs 'is'.

63
00:04:10.433 --> 00:04:13.086
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:13.900 --> 00:04:15.249
The Limits of the Mind.

65
00:04:15.549 --> 00:04:19.685
Cognitive science treats the mind as an information-processing system.

66
00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:24.963
Perception builds a mental representation of the world from noisy sensory data.

67
00:04:25.967 --> 00:04:32.053
Learning is often constrained by cognitive load, since working memory can hold only a handful of items at once.

68
00:04:33.067 --> 00:04:39.292
Attention is limited by fatigue, and memory encoding is constrained by how meaningful the material appears.

69
00:04:40.300 --> 00:04:45.388
Teachers can therefore improve results by chunking content and removing unnecessary detail.

70
00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:50.896
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:51.750 --> 00:04:54.644
Learning is often constrained blank cognitive load.

72
00:04:54.944 --> 00:04:57.436
A, by. B, from. C, of.

73
00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:02.593
The answer is A: by.

74
00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:06.527
The mental effort used in working memory is blank.

75
00:05:06.827 --> 00:05:10.945
A, cognitive load. B, perception. C, encoding.

76
00:05:14.500 --> 00:05:16.906
The answer is A: cognitive load.

77
00:05:18.050 --> 00:05:21.036
Turning experience into a storable memory is blank.

78
00:05:21.336 --> 00:05:25.454
A, attention. B, memory encoding. C, cognition.

79
00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:31.336
The answer is B: memory encoding.

80
00:05:32.483 --> 00:05:34.216
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:34.516 --> 00:05:41.606
A, Attention limited by fatigue.. B, Attention is limited by fatigue.. C, Attention is limit by fatigue..

82
00:05:45.167 --> 00:05:48.826
The answer is B: Attention is limited by fatigue..

83
00:05:49.983 --> 00:05:53.109
According to the reading, teachers can improve results by…

84
00:05:53.409 --> 00:05:58.200
A, adding detail. B, chunking content. C, lengthening classes.

85
00:06:01.767 --> 00:06:04.498
The answer is B: chunking content.

86
00:06:05.700 --> 00:06:08.770
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:09.617 --> 00:06:13.462
What strategies help reduce cognitive load when learning something new?

88
00:06:30.250 --> 00:06:31.692
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:31.992 --> 00:06:56.477
Breaking material into small chunks helps a great deal, because working memory is limited by capacity. I also remove distractions, study in short sessions and use examples before abstract rules. Linking new ideas to what I already know reduces the effort needed to encode them. Testing myself afterwards strengthens memory.

90
00:06:56.777 --> 00:06:58.823
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:59.717 --> 00:07:04.772
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and constrained by and limited by.

92
00:07:05.072 --> 00:07:09.269
Today's value is self-awareness. Know your strengths and keep growing.

93
00:07:09.569 --> 00:07:11.850
See you in Unit five. Goodbye!

94
00:07:12.150 --> 00:07:15.882
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

